.TH RETICLE 1 "2026-08-27" "reticle 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
reticle \- an Emacs-class editor for Verilog and SystemVerilog RTL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B reticle
[\fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-nw\fR|\fB\-\-tui\fR|\fB\-\-gui\fR] [\fB\-q\fR]
.br
.B reticle
\fB\-\-repl\fR
.br
.B reticle
\fB\-\-eval\fR \fIEXPR\fR
.br
.B reticle
\fB\-\-script\fR \fIFILE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B reticle
is an Emacs-like editor implemented in Rust, with a self-contained Elisp
interpreter (bytecode VM plus a native JIT for hot functions), both a
terminal (TUI) and a graphical (GUI) front end, tree-sitter based syntax
highlighting, an LSP client, and a C ABI mechanism for dynamically loaded
modules. Its feature priorities are tuned for Verilog/SystemVerilog RTL
development.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I FILE
A file to open on startup. Only meaningful for a GUI or TUI session (i.e.
when none of \fB\-\-repl\fR, \fB\-\-eval\fR, or \fB\-\-script\fR is given).
.TP
.B \-nw, \-\-tui
Run in the terminal instead of opening a GUI window. This flag is
recognized anywhere on the command line, and always wins over
.B \-\-gui
no matter which one appears first or last.
.TP
.B \-\-gui
Run the GUI editor window. This is the default when neither
.B \-nw
nor
.B \-\-tui
is present; spelling it out explicitly does not override
.BR \-nw / \-\-tui .
.TP
.B \-q
Skip loading the user init file for this session.
.TP
.B \-\-repl
Start an interactive Elisp REPL on stdin/stdout. Like
.B \-\-eval
and
.BR \-\-script ,
this only takes effect when it is the very first command-line argument.
Elsewhere on the command line it has one of two fates depending on
position: in a GUI/TUI flag position it is rejected as an unknown option
(since it begins with a dash); in the value slot right after another
.B \-\-eval
or
.B \-\-script
(i.e. the second argument), or as a trailing argument after
.BR \-\-repl / \-\-eval\ EXPR / \-\-script\ FILE ,
it is silently ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-eval \fIEXPR\fR
Evaluate the Elisp expression \fIEXPR\fR and print the result, then exit.
Must be the first argument, immediately followed by \fIEXPR\fR as the
next argument.
.TP
.B \-\-script \fIFILE\fR
Load and evaluate the Elisp file \fIFILE\fR, then exit. Must be the
first argument, immediately followed by \fIFILE\fR as the next argument.
.TP
.B \-\-worker
Internal use only. Runs the process as a worker subprocess that reads
framed s\-expressions from stdin, evaluates them, and writes framed
results to stdout until the parent closes stdin. It is spawned
automatically (re\-executing the same binary) by the editor's background
worker facility \-\- it is not meant to be invoked by hand.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a help summary and exit successfully. Takes priority over every
other argument in a flag position \-\- including before \fB\-\-repl\fR,
\fB\-\-eval\fR, or \fB\-\-script\fR, so it can never be shadowed by an editor
session, REPL, or evaluation that would otherwise run first. The one
position where it is not a flag is the value slot right after
\fB\-\-eval\fR or \fB\-\-script\fR: \fBreticle \-\-eval \-\-help\fR
evaluates \fB\-\-help\fR as elisp and fails, rather than printing this
help. The same applies to \fB\-v\fR, \fB\-V\fR, and \fB\-\-version\fR.
.TP
.B \-v, \-V, \-\-version
Print version information and exit successfully. Same priority rule as
.BR \-h / \-\-help .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: this includes
.BR \-\-help / \-\-version ,
a successful
.B \-\-eval
or
.BR \-\-script ,
and a REPL session that ends on normal end\-of\-file (Ctrl\-D).
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error: a GUI error, a terminal error, a failed evaluation
during
.BR \-\-eval / \-\-script ,
or a stdin I/O error while reading REPL input.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unrecognized option, or
.B \-\-eval
/
.B \-\-script
given with no following argument.
.TP
.B 101
The process panicked (Rust's default panic exit status).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RETICLE_REMOTE_TIMEOUT
Overrides how many seconds a remote command started over ssh is allowed
to run before it is treated as timed out; accepts a floating\-point
number of seconds. A value of zero or less disables the timeout
entirely. A positive value is clamped to the range
.RI [ 0.001 ", " 86400 ]
seconds (i.e. anything above 86400 becomes 86400, and anything below
0.001 but still positive becomes 0.001). Defaults to 30 seconds when
unset, non\-finite, or unparsable.
.TP
.B RETICLE_SSH_BIN
Overrides which \fBssh\fR binary is invoked for remote operations;
defaults to \fBssh\fR on the \fBPATH\fR. Primarily useful for pointing
tests at a local shim instead of a real ssh client.
.TP
.B RETICLE_WORKER_EXE
Overrides the path to the \fBreticle\fR executable used to spawn
.B \-\-worker
background subprocesses; defaults to the currently running executable
.RI ( "std::env::current_exe" ).
Primarily useful for integration tests that build their own copy of the
binary.
.TP
.B JIT_DEBUG
When set to any value, enables diagnostic output from the native JIT
compiler (compiled IR, per\-instruction traces, and reasons a function
was rejected for compilation) on stderr. Unset by default.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.reticle/init.el
The user init file, loaded on startup unless
.B \-q
is given. If it does not exist, startup proceeds without error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR emacs (1)
